{"error":false,"activeTest":false,"task":{"id":14228211,"type":"Review","status":"Processed","importance":1,"reason":null,"locked":null,"proofId":null,"currentQuestionId":null,"currentQuestionTimestamp":null,"progress":1,"points":4,"passed":1,"rawPointsAwarded":6,"pointsAwarded":6,"mp":null,"mpAwarded":null,"mpDecayRate":null,"student":{"id":57975,"firstName":"Harrison","lastName":"Charles","locale":null,"subscriptionId":"sub_1UO3yHD4gcTFRx97dYjQXmX1","sequenceId":19},"course":{"id":111,"name":"Mathematical Foundations II"},"topic":{"id":942,"name":"Closure Properties of Polynomials"},"test":{"id":null,"name":null,"diagnostic":null,"timeLimit":null,"questionCount":null},"created":"2026-10-15T05:11:42.000Z","started":"2026-10-15T06:21:58.000Z","completed":"2026-10-15T06:22:47.000Z","processed":"2026-10-15T06:22:58.000Z","progressStr":"100%","answers":[{"id":62204178,"created":"2026-10-15T06:22:13.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":15019,"number":1,"createdStr":"Thu, Oct 15th @ 6:22 AM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:15","question":{"id":46426,"format":"MC","difficulty":3,"exampleId":1318,"text":"<p>If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $5$, respectively, which of the following statements are true?</p>\n<ol class=\"questionStatements\"><li>  $P + Q$ is always a polynomial </li><li>  $P \\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial </li><li>  $\\dfrac{P}{Q}$ is always a polynomial </li></ol>","choice1":"I and II only","choice2":"I, II, and III","choice3":"II and III only","choice4":"III only","choice5":"None","freeAnswerChoice":" ","explanation":"<p>Let&#39;s look at each statement in turn.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under addition.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under multiplication.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P=x^3$ and $Q=x^5.$ Then $\\dfrac{P}{Q} = \\dfrac{x^3}{x^5}= \\dfrac{1}{x^2},$ which is not a polynomial.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In conclusion, only statements I and II are true.</p>\n","topic":{"id":942,"name":"Closure Properties of Polynomials"},"example":{"id":1318,"title":"Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"},"group":{"id":10135,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[{"id":null,"number":null,"answers":[]}],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14228211,"answer":{"id":62204178,"cread":"2026-10-15T06:22:13.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":15019},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyHard","difficultySymbol":"H","rendered":true,"testMode":false}},{"id":62204216,"created":"2026-10-15T06:22:30.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":13352,"number":2,"createdStr":"Thu, Oct 15th @ 6:22 AM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:13","question":{"id":51691,"format":"MC","difficulty":3,"exampleId":1318,"text":"<p>Given that $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $5,$ which of the following statements are always true?</p>\n<ol class=\"questionStatements\"><li>  $P(x) - 4x^6$ is a polynomial </li><li>  $4x^6\\cdot P(x)$ is a polynomial </li><li>  $\\dfrac{4x^6}{P(x)}$ is a polynomial </li></ol>","choice1":"I and II only","choice2":"I and III only","choice3":"II and III only","choice4":"I only","choice5":"II only","freeAnswerChoice":"","explanation":"<p>Let&#39;s look at each statement in turn.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under subtraction.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under multiplication.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P(x)=x^5+1.$ Then $\\dfrac{4x^6}{P(x)}= \\dfrac{4x^6}{x^5+1},$ which is not a polynomial.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In conclusion, only statements I and II are true.</p>\n","topic":{"id":942,"name":"Closure Properties of Polynomials"},"example":{"id":1318,"title":"Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"},"group":{"id":44268,"ca